 "pg.1a6ff8f0dc": "<b>Each class against its own null</b>, measured on the same {n0} star-sectors: what its rule finds where its signal cannot be. Dips: the flux turned upside down (a real dip becomes a bump; a real flare or pulsation maximum becomes a dip, so this null is not empty of real signals). Flares: downward events with the real dips masked. Variability: a red-noise copy of the star (AR({n1}) fitted to its bins) through the same rule, which needs the same best period (within {n2}%) in both halves of the light curve. Of its periodogram peaks not at a binary&#x27;s period, {n3} are coherent; unclassified are {n4} longer than a half can test (coherence not tested), {n5} at the edge of the period grid and {n6} that wander between halves. Limits: it finds a {n7} ppm sinusoid in strong red noise only {n8}/{n9} times (scripts/variability_rule_check.py), and cannot test periods longer than about {n10} d in one sector. Kind is compared with kind, star-sector is the unit: the {n11}% interval is a paired bootstrap over star-sectors, beside McNemar's test. A class that does not pass both is not distinguishable from noise in this slice: Planet candidate, Eclipsing binary, Flare, Pulsator or rotational variable, Systematic. Of the {n12} flare-class brightenings, {n13} rise faster than they decay and {n14} the other way ({n15} equal); real flares rise fast, noise has no preferred direction.",
