 "pg.9abb111076": "Four coronagraph families as optical trains, each in four planes with a log-intensity profile below. Lyot: an occulting disc blocks the star&#x27;s image, the light it diffracts lands on the rim of the re-imaged pupil, and a closing Lyot stop removes that rim so the starlight beside the mask falls by a factor of hundreds. Apodised pupil: the pupil&#x27;s transmission tapers to its edge, the image&#x27;s rings fade and the core widens. Vortex: a phase ramp that winds twice around the centre throws all of an on-axis star&#x27;s light outside the pupil, where the stop removes it. Adaptive optics with angular differential imaging: synthetic frames in which speckles stay fixed while the planet turns with the sky; the median of the frames is subtracted, each frame is turned back and the stack reveals the planet.",
