 "pg.b8ff884f19": "A molecule with N atoms has 3N − {n0} ways to vibrate (3N − {n1} if it is straight): water has three, carbon dioxide four (its bend comes twice, at right angles), methane nine. A mode absorbs infrared light only if it changes the molecule's dipole. That is why CO₂'s symmetric stretch ({n2} µm), O₂'s stretch and two of methane's four fundamentals are silent in the infrared. The bands the modes make are the ones the Reading atmospheres chapter marks: water's two stretches at {n3} and {n4} µm make its {n5} µm band, methane's ν₃ the {n6} µm band, CO₂'s asymmetric stretch the {n7} µm band, and CO's stretch the {n8} µm band.",
